.TH MULL 5 "November 9, 2026" "MuLL" "File Formats and Libraries"
.SH NAME
mull \- MuLL standard-library packages, interfaces, and contracts
.SH DESCRIPTION
The standard library contains five native base packages and thirty portable
MuLL packages, shared by the C++ and D hosts. The optional Mulib provider adds
a separate native package. Package names and member names are case-sensitive.
The command-line hosts register the available native packages automatically;
embedding applications call mull::stdlib::install or mull.stdlib.Stdlib.install.
.SH DISCOVERY AND IMPORTS
For each configured root, the loader searches root/name/Package.mull in order.
Names contain ASCII letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens.
Successful packages must return records and are cached by runtime and name.
Failures return nil and set the import diagnostic;
recursive imports are rejected.
An existing package's parse/evaluation failure does not fall through
to a later root.
Success, including a cache hit, clears the diagnostic.
.PP
Packages run in isolated environments containing G. Returned closures can retain
private state. Native Package.mull facades return G:Native("name"); registration
alone does not replace the discoverable package file.
.nf
val P <- G:LoadLib("printf") or G:DieWith("Import Error: $E?");
P:SetMainSink(G:GetStdout());
P:Println("ready");
.fi
.PP
Use mull -I DIR to add discovery roots; MULL_PATH follows explicit roots.
The installed root is normally PREFIX/share/mull/stdlib.
Package discovery is not native shared-library loading. Native -L/-l options
and --extern bindings are described in mull(1).
.SH COMMON VALUE CONTRACTS
Numbers are finite IEEE binary64 values. No implicit string/number coercion is
performed. Only nil and false are falsey; zero and empty lists/strings are true.
Collections are structurally immutable; updating functions return new values.
Equality is structural except callable/foreign identities.
Strings and indices are byte-oriented, not Unicode-character-oriented.
Indices and counts are integral and checked; invalid preconditions throw unless
an API explicitly documents a fallback or Clamp operation.
Arguments evaluate eagerly and left to right; library functions cannot make an
already-evaluated argument lazy.
.SH CORE HOST RECORD
.TP
.B G:LoadLib(name), G:Native(name)
Discover a package, or retrieve a registered native package implementation.
.TP
.B G:GetStdout(), G:DieWith(value), G:Assert(value)
Obtain the current output callable, raise an error,
or reject a falsey assertion.
.TP
.B G:Str(value), G:Type(value), G:Len(value)
Display text, type name, or byte/list/record length. Display text is not a
serialization format; numeric display uses seventeen significant digits.
.TP
.B G:MulibWrap(name)
Obtain a typed namespace from the optional MuLIB provider; unavailable providers
and unknown libraries raise an error.
.SH NATIVE BASE PACKAGES
.TP
.B printf
SetMainSink(fn), Format(template,...), Printf(template,...),
Print(...), Println(...).
Format substitutes {} placeholders, with {{ and }} for literal braces and exact
argument counts. Print functions concatenate without separators;
Println adds LF.
Sinks take one string; output functions return nil. Recursive writes through
the same sink are rejected. A sink can replace itself for subsequent writes.
.TP
.B terminal
Write(string), Clear(), Color(code,string). Write/Clear use the runtime stdout
callback directly; Clear emits ANSI control bytes.
Color returns a string wrapped
in ANSI SGR for integral codes 0 through 107; it does not write it.
.TP
.B math
Abs(x), Floor(x), Ceil(x), Sqrt(x), Pow(x,y), Min(x,y), Max(x,y), Pi.
Nonfinite results and invalid numeric operations are errors.
.TP
.B text
Split(s,separator), Join(strings,separator), Replace(s,from,to),
Upper(s), Lower(s).
Split preserves empty fields. Empty separators/search strings are rejected.
Replace is nonoverlapping; case conversion is ASCII-only.
.TP
.B list
Range(start,end), Map(xs,fn), Filter(xs,fn), Fold(xs,initial,fn),
Push(xs,value),
Set(xs,index,value). Range is ascending, integral, end-exclusive, within
plus/minus (2^53-1), with at most one million elements.
Callbacks receive one element, or accumulator then element for Fold,
in list order.
Push/Set return new lists; Set requires an existing index.
.SH COMPOSITION AND DATA
.TP
.B option
Some(x), None(), GetOr(o,fallback), Map(o,f), Bind(o,f).
Records contain present and value. Some(nil) is present.
Map wraps its callback's
result; Bind returns the callback result directly. Fallback arguments are eager.
.TP
.B result
Ok(x), Err(e), Map(r,f), Bind(r,f), Unwrap(r).
Records contain ok, value, error. Failed results bypass callbacks; Unwrap raises
the stored error. These functions do not catch runtime exceptions.
.TP
.B function
Identity(x), Constant(x), Compose(f,g), Flip(f), Partial(f,a), Times(n,f,x).
Composition is f(g(x)); Flip/Partial operate on binary functions.
Times iterates a unary function with a nonnegative integral count.
.TP
.B predicate
Negate(p), Both(p,q), Either(p,q), All(xs,p), Any(xs,p).
Both/Either preserve operand-valued short-circuit results. All/Any return
Booleans; All of an empty list is true and Any of an empty list is false.
.TP
.B pair
New(a,b), First(p), Second(p), Swap(p), Map(p,f,g).
Pairs are lists of exactly two elements; consumers check that length.
.TP
.B sequence
Zip(a,b), Enumerate(xs), Flatten(xs), Reverse(xs).
Zip truncates to the shorter input; enumeration is zero-based; Flatten removes
exactly one list level.
.TP
.B slice
Sub(xs,start,end), Take(xs,n), Drop(xs,n), Chunk(xs,n).
Sub uses checked half-open integral bounds. Chunk size is positive; the last
chunk may be shorter.
.TP
.B search
Index(xs,value), Contains(xs,value), Find(xs,p), Count(xs,p).
Index returns nil when absent. Find returns a record with found, index, value,
distinguishing an actual matched nil. The first match wins;
equality is structural.
.TP
.B sort
By(xs,less), Ascending(xs), Descending(xs).
Stable insertion sorting; less must define a strict ordering.
Ascending/Descending are numeric; input is unchanged.
.TP
.B set
Unique(xs), Union(a,b), Intersection(a,b), Difference(a,b).
Lists represent sets with structural equality, unique results, and stable
first-occurrence order.
.TP
.B stack
New(), Push(xs,x), Peek(xs), Pop(xs). The last element is top.
Pop returns a record with value and rest; Peek/Pop reject empty input.
.TP
.B queue
New(), Enqueue(xs,x), Peek(xs), Dequeue(xs). The first element is front.
Dequeue returns value and rest; Peek/Dequeue reject empty input.
.TP
.B deque
New(), PushFront(xs,x), PushBack(xs,x), PopFront(xs), PopBack(xs).
Functional double-ended queue; removals return value and rest
and reject emptiness.
.SH NUMBERS AND COLLECTIONS
.TP
.B vector
Dot(a,b), Scale(a,k), Norm(a), Add(a,b), Normalize(a).
Numeric lists; binary operations require equal lengths. Empty dot is zero;
normalization rejects zero norm.
.TP
.B matrix
Shape(m), Transpose(m), Multiply(a,b).
Rectangular lists of rows; shape is [rows,columns]. Empty outer lists have shape
[0,0] and cannot retain a nonzero column count.
Multiplication requires compatible
dimensions and numeric entries.
.TP
.B statistics
Sum(xs), Mean(xs), Variance(xs), Median(xs).
Variance is population variance (divide by n); median averages the middle pair.
Empty sum is zero; the other functions reject emptiness.
Binary64 rounding applies.
.TP
.B integer
Check(x), Gcd(a,b), Lcm(a,b), DivMod(a,b), IsEven(x).
Checked exact integers within plus/minus (2^53-1); LCM checks its result.
GCD/LCM are nonnegative; gcd(0,0) is zero.
DivMod uses floor quotient and remainder
with the divisor's sign; zero divisor is rejected.
.TP
.B bit
And(a,b), Or(a,b), Xor(a,b), Not(a), ShiftLeft(a,n), ShiftRight(a,n).
Unsigned 32-bit inputs; integral shift counts 0 through 32. Left shift wraps
modulo 2^32; right shift is logical. A shift by 32 returns zero.
.TP
.B combinatorics
Factorial(n), Choose(n,k), Cartesian(a,b).
Factorial accepts 0 through 18; Choose accepts 0<=k<=n<=50,
keeping intermediates
exact. Cartesian order is left-list-major; an empty input yields empty output.
.TP
.B range
Step(start,end,step), Contains(start,end,x), Clamp(x,low,high).
Step uses checked exact integers and a nonzero signed step, excluding the end;
a direction mismatch yields empty output. Contains is half-open. Clamp requires
low<=high.
.SH STRINGS AND APPLICATION UTILITIES
.TP
.B string
Sub(s,start,end), StartsWith(s,p), EndsWith(s,p), Trim(s), Repeat(s,n).
Sub uses checked integral half-open byte bounds. Trim removes space/TAB/CR/LF.
Empty prefixes/suffixes match. Repeat count is nonnegative integral.
.TP
.B ascii
Digits, Upper, Lower, IsDigit(c), IsAlpha(c), IsAlnum(c), IsSpace(c), Digit(c).
Predicates require one byte. Whitespace means space/TAB/CR/LF. Digit accepts
a decimal ASCII digit and returns 0 through 9.
.TP
.B csv
Encode(rows), Decode(s).
String fields, commas, doubled-quote escaping. Decode accepts LF/CR/CRLF and
quoted embedded newlines, rejecting malformed quotes. Encode quotes every field
and terminates every nonempty row with LF. Empty document is []; an empty field
is an empty string.
.TP
.B path
Normalize(p), Join(a,b), Basename(p), Dirname(p).
POSIX lexical operations, not filesystem access. Dot segments and repeated
slashes are removed, unresolved relative .. is preserved, and absolute paths
stay rooted. Empty normalizes to a dot. Absolute b replaces a in Join.
.TP
.B url
Encode(s), Decode(s).
Percent coding for printable ASCII plus TAB/CR/LF.
Unreserved bytes pass through;
plus is literal. Decode accepts either hex case. Malformed/truncated escapes or
unsupported bytes are errors. This is not a full URL parser or Unicode codec.
.TP
.B semver
Parse(s), Compare(a,b), Compatible(a,b).
Strict major.minor.patch cores, exact integers, no leading zeros or suffixes.
Compare returns -1, 0, or 1. Compatibility means same nonzero major,
same nonzero minor under major zero, or identical patch under 0.0.
It is symmetric, not a range.
.TP
.B units
Radians(degrees), Degrees(radians), CelsiusToFahrenheit(c),
FahrenheitToCelsius(f), MetersToFeet(m), FeetToMeters(f),
SecondsToMilliseconds(s), MillisecondsToSeconds(ms).
Scalar binary64 conversions; one foot is exactly 0.3048 meters by definition.
.TP
.B geometry
Point(x,y), Rect(x,y,w,h), Distance(a,b), Area(r), Contains(r,p),
Intersection(a,b).
Records with x/y and width/height. Extents are nonnegative; right/bottom edges
are excluded. Disjoint intersection has zero extent.
.TP
.B color
Rgb(r,g,b), Hex(c), Mix(a,b,t), Invert(c).
Integral channels 0 through 255; uppercase #RRGGBB. Mix uses t in [0,1] and
nearest-integer rounding, without gamma correction.
.TP
.B assert
Check(condition,message), Equal(a,b), NotEqual(a,b), Near(a,b,tolerance).
Failures throw through G:DieWith/G:Assert.
Near uses nonnegative absolute tolerance.
Successful checks return the first value or condition.
.SH OPTIONAL MULIB PROVIDER
.B Mulib
provides Wrap(name), Libraries(), New(type,initial?), Array(type,countOrList),
Buffer(sizeOrString), Callback(type,fn), SizeOf(type).
It is present only when built with the optional MuLIB bridge. Wrapped values
have a distinct mulib type, runtime ownership, native storage, and callback
lifetime rules. It is not the narrow --extern double-function ABI.
See the installed MuLIB bridge reference for supported typed adapters.
.SH COST AND LIMITS
Portable packages favor small reusable algorithms. Repeated immutable appends
copy accumulated output; many builders are quadratic. Set membership is linear;
insertion sort is quadratic in comparisons with additional copying costs.
Square matrix multiplication uses cubic scalar work. Use native packages for
large workloads. All MuLL loops and script callbacks share configured step,
call-depth, environment, and collection-depth budgets; arbitrary native code
is outside those work/time bounds. Mu3AC adds a bounded value-handle arena.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/mull/stdlib/*/Package.mull
Discoverable package implementations or native facades.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/doc/MuDSL/mull/stdlib/README.md
Expanded source catalogue and detailed package contracts.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mull (1)
